--TEST--
`Core\Cli::displayWidth` is the count a column of text aligns on, asserted over a sweep of labels rather than off one line: padding to a column count aligns every one of them, padding to a cluster count aligns only the labels where the two measures happen to agree — `rule:tooling/the-terminal-profile-resolves-once`, `rule:types/string-is-utf8`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// § 3 sites this member on `Core\Cli` and not on `Core\Str` because a column
// count is a property of the renderer: `rule:types/string-is-utf8` fixed `string`'s own unit
// as the grapheme cluster, and a terminal does not draw clusters, it draws
// columns. This case is that difference made observable, and it is asserted by
// **counting** the labels that came out ten columns wide — a member answering
// plausibly on the ASCII row would still fail here.
//
// The four labels are chosen so the two measures agree on half of them: `ok`
// and a combining-mark `café` are one column per cluster, while a CJK
// ideograph and a fullwidth Latin letter are two. A padding that used the
// wrong unit therefore still looks right on two rows out of four, which is
// exactly how this bug survives a hand-checked table.
array<string> $labels = ["ok", "日本", "ｗｉｄｅ", "cafe" . Core\Str::fromCodePoint(769)];

uint $byColumn = 0;
uint $byCluster = 0;
foreach ($labels as string $label) {
    string $padded = $label . Core\Str::repeat(" ", 10 - Core\Cli::displayWidth($label));
    if (Core\Cli::displayWidth($padded) == 10) {
        $byColumn = $byColumn + 1;
    }
    // `padEnd` counts what `Core\Str::length` counts, which is the point.
    if (Core\Cli::displayWidth(Core\Str::padEnd($label, 10)) == 10) {
        $byCluster = $byCluster + 1;
    }
}

echo "labels=", Core\Arr::count($labels), "\n";
echo "aligned by column count=", $byColumn, "\n";
echo "aligned by cluster count=", $byCluster, "\n";

// The same divergence on one row, so the counts above have a witness: two
// clusters, four columns.
echo "clusters=", Core\Str::length("日本"), " columns=", Core\Cli::displayWidth("日本"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
labels=4
aligned by column count=4
aligned by cluster count=2
clusters=2 columns=4
